Marks the author places on their own words — emphasis and the small typographic and semantic marks.

<section #sh-abbr | `<abbr>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *abbr*. Compiles to eHTML `<abbr>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<abbr>`

<code #code:sh-abbr-1>
The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM> is the browser API for HTML.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-abbr-1>
The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM> is the browser API for HTML.
</minipage>

*Standard pattern — abbreviation with its expansion as the title
kwarg. Browsers show the expansion in a hover tooltip.*

<code #code:sh-abbr-2>
Using <abbr | CSS> selectors.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-abbr-2>
Using <abbr | CSS> selectors.
</minipage>

*Bare abbreviation, no title. Acceptable when the expansion
has already been introduced earlier in the document.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | kwarg |  | The abbreviation's expansion. |

<section #sh-b | `<b>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *b*. Compiles to eHTML `<b>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<b>`
- Markdown: `**…**`

<code #code:sh-b-1>
The keyword <b type=keyword | recursion> is fundamental.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-b-1>
The keyword <b type=keyword | recursion> is fundamental.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-b-2>
Use <b type=product-name | Acrobat> to read the file.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-b-2>
Use <b type=product-name | Acrobat> to read the file.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `keyword` `product-name` `lead` `offset` `other` |  |

<section #sh-em | `<em>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *em*. Compiles to eHTML `<em>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<em>`

<code #code:sh-em-1>
This has *emphasized* content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-em-1>
This has *emphasized* content.
</minipage>

*Plain markdown with single asterisks (or single underscores) produces the
visual &lt;i&gt;, not the semantic &lt;em&gt; — markdown emphasis maps to the visual
tag. &lt;em&gt; is reached only by the explicit form.*

<code #code:sh-em-2>
<em | emphasized>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-em-2>
<em | emphasized>
</minipage>

*The explicit form is reached for when attributes are needed.*

<code #code:sh-em-3>
<em #key-term .highlighted | distinguishing feature>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-em-3>
<em #key-term .highlighted | distinguishing feature>
</minipage>

<section #sh-i | `<i>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *i*. Compiles to eHTML `<i>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<i>`
- Markdown: `*…*` (or `_…_`)

<code #code:sh-i-1>
The species is <i type=taxonomic | Loxodonta africana>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-i-1>
The species is <i type=taxonomic | Loxodonta africana>.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-i-2>
The French <i type=foreign | tour de force> is impressive.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-i-2>
The French <i type=foreign | tour de force> is impressive.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-i-3>
The technical term <i type=technical | mitochondria> refers to organelles.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-i-3>
The technical term <i type=technical | mitochondria> refers to organelles.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `foreign` `taxonomic` `technical` `thought` `ship-name` `other` | Optional classification of the italic's role. |

<section #sh-kbd | `<kbd>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *kbd*. Compiles to eHTML `<kbd>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<kbd>`

<code #code:sh-kbd-1>
Press <kbd | Ctrl+C> to copy.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-kbd-1>
Press <kbd | Ctrl+C> to copy.
</minipage>

*Single chord as a kbd block. Browsers render &lt;kbd&gt; in a
monospace font by default, distinguishing it from surrounding
prose.*

<code #code:sh-kbd-2>
Press <kbd | <kbd | Ctrl>+<kbd | C>> to copy.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-kbd-2>
Press <kbd | <kbd | Ctrl>+<kbd | C>> to copy.
</minipage>

*Nested &lt;kbd&gt; distinguishes individual keys in a chord. Browsers
render the outer block as the chord and the inner blocks as
individual keys, both monospace.*

<section #sh-output | `<output>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *output*. Compiles to eHTML `<output>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<output>`

<code #code:sh-output-1>
The function returns <output | 42> for the test input.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-output-1>
The function returns <output | 42> for the test input.
</minipage>

*Result of a calculation in prose. &lt;output&gt; is semantically
distinct from &lt;samp&gt; — &lt;samp&gt; is what a program prints (a
display artifact); &lt;output&gt; is the result of a computation
(a semantic value). Browsers render both similarly.*

<section #sh-s | `<s>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *s*. Compiles to eHTML `<s>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<s>`
- Markdown: `~~…~~`

<code #code:sh-s-1>
The price is ~~$50~~ now $40.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-s-1>
The price is ~~$50~~ now $40.
</minipage>

*GFM's tilde syntax produces &lt;s&gt; elements. The most common
authoring path for casual strikethrough.*

<code #code:sh-s-2>
The claim <s type=retracted | was unsupported> has been corrected.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-s-2>
The claim <s type=retracted | was unsupported> has been corrected.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `outdated` `retracted` `deleted` `other` |  |

<section #sh-samp | `<samp>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *samp*. Compiles to eHTML `<samp>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<samp>`

<code #code:sh-samp-1>
The command prints <samp | Hello, world!> to stdout.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-samp-1>
The command prints <samp | Hello, world!> to stdout.
</minipage>

*Sample output from a program. Browsers render &lt;samp&gt; in a
monospace font by default, distinguishing it from surrounding
prose.*

<code #code:sh-samp-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-samp-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</minipage>

*&lt;samp&gt; for the sample value paired with &lt;var&gt; for the variable
name — the natural pair for documenting configuration in
technical writing.*

<section #sh-strong | `<strong>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *strong*. Compiles to eHTML `<strong>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<strong>`

<code #code:sh-strong-1>
This is **strongly emphasized** content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-strong-1>
This is **strongly emphasized** content.
</minipage>

*Plain markdown with double asterisks (or double underscores) produces the
visual &lt;b&gt;, not the semantic &lt;strong&gt; — markdown emphasis maps to the
visual tag. &lt;strong&gt; is reached only by the explicit form.*

<code #code:sh-strong-2>
<strong | important>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-strong-2>
<strong | important>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-strong-3>
<strong #critical .warning | This is critical.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-strong-3>
<strong #critical .warning | This is critical.>
</minipage>

<section #sh-sub | `<sub>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *sub*. Compiles to eHTML `<sub>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<sub>`

<code #code:sh-sub-1>
Water is H<sub | 2>O.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-1>
Water is H<sub | 2>O.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sub-2>
The vector x<sub | i> represents the i-th component.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-2>
The vector x<sub | i> represents the i-th component.
</minipage>

<section #sh-sup | `<sup>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *sup*. Compiles to eHTML `<sup>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<sup>`

<code #code:sh-sup-1>
The 1<sup | st> edition of the work.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sup-1>
The 1<sup | st> edition of the work.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sup-2>
The function f(x) = x<sup | 2>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sup-2>
The function f(x) = x<sup | 2>.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sup-3>
The isotope <sup | 12>C is abundant.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sup-3>
The isotope <sup | 12>C is abundant.
</minipage>

<section #sh-term | `<term>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *term*. Compiles to eHTML `<term>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<term>`

<code #code:sh-term-1>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-term-1>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation.
</minipage>

*Standard term introduction. The element marks the word as
"this is a term being introduced," typically rendered in italic
or bold by default CSS.*

<code #code:sh-term-2>
An <term #term:eigenvector | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation. Later we generalize <term | eigenvector>s to operators.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-term-2>
An <term #term:eigenvector | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation. Later we generalize <term | eigenvector>s to operators.
</minipage>

*First introduction carries an id so later references can link
back to it. Subsequent uses of the same term (without an id)
still mark it as a term being referenced, distinct from running
prose, without re-asserting the introduction.*

<section #sh-u | `<u>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *u*. Compiles to eHTML `<u>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<u>`

<code #code:sh-u-1>
The author wrote <u type=misspelling | recieve> in the original.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-u-1>
The author wrote <u type=misspelling | recieve> in the original.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-u-2>
The Chinese name <u type=proper-name | 王明> appears here.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-u-2>
The Chinese name <u type=proper-name | 王明> appears here.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `misspelling` `proper-name` `editorial-correction` `other` |  |

<section #sh-var | `<var>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *var*. Compiles to eHTML `<var>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<var>`

<code #code:sh-var-1>
The function takes a parameter <var | n> and returns <var | n>²+1.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-var-1>
The function takes a parameter <var | n> and returns <var | n>²+1.
</minipage>

*Variable names in prose. Browsers render &lt;var&gt; in italic by
default, distinguishing it from surrounding prose.*

<code #code:sh-var-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-var-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</minipage>

*&lt;var&gt; for the variable name and &lt;samp&gt; for a sample value —
the natural pair for documenting configuration in technical writing.*
